<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christopher Marlowe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1564">1564</a>–<a href="/years/1593">1593</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christopher Marlowe was an English playwright and poet of the Elizabethan age whose plays Tamburlaine, Doctor Faustus, The Jew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and Edward II established blank verse as the main form of English drama. Educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, he was linked to government secret service and was killed in a quarrel at Deptford in 1593, aged 29. His work strongly influenced the young Shakespeare.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christopher-marlowe.jpg" alt="Christopher Marlowe" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christopher_Marlowe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Elizabethan and Jacobean age produced the drama of William Shakespeare, <b>Christopher Marlowe</b> and Ben Jonson, and the King James Bible of 1611 shaped English prose for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Deptford, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.28,1.08&amp;year=1582" aria-label="Canterbury on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4574 387 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christopher Marlowe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5024 657h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5024 657h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.28,1.08&amp;year=1582">What was here in 1582? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Was this the face that launch'd a thousand ships, And burnt the topless towers of Ilium?— Sweet Helen, make me immortal with a kiss.&#8221;</p><footer>Doctor Faustus, act 5 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Faustus to the spirit of Helen of Troy &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/811/pg811-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Hell hath no limits, nor is circumscrib'd In one self-place; but where we are is hell, And where hell is, there must we ever be…&#8221;</p><footer>Doctor Faustus, act 2 &#183; original &#183; spoken by the devil Mephistophilis &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/811/pg811-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The stars move still, time runs, the clock will strike, The devil will come, and Faustus must be damn'd.&#8221;</p><footer>Doctor Faustus, act 5 (the last hour) &#183; original &#183; spoken by Faustus as midnight nears &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/811/pg811-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Why, this is hell, nor am I out of it: Think'st thou that I, that saw the face of God, And tasted the eternal joys of heaven, Am not tormented with ten thousand hells, In being depriv'd of everlasting bliss?&#8221;</p><footer>Doctor Faustus, act 1 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Mephistophilis to Faustus &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/811/pg811-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Doth teach us all to have aspiring minds: Our souls, whose faculties can comprehend The wondrous architecture of the world, And measure every wandering planet's course, Still climbing after knowledge infinite, And always moving as the restless spheres…&#8221;</p><footer>Tamburlaine the Great, part 1 (c. 1587), act 2 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Tamburlaine &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1094/pg1094-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
